The wind was a wild thing that night, tearing at the lighthouse glass and howling through the iron ribs of the structure. Elias, his beard a frosted thicket, wrestled the heavy door shut against the gale. He was a man carved from solitude, his face a map of squints and sea-spray. The storm had been his only company for three days.

On the fourth morning, the sea was a sullen, grey beast, licking its wounds. Elias made his customary circuit of the rocky spit, his seaboots crunching on kelp and shattered shell. It was then he saw it, wedged between two jagged teeth of rock—a dark green bottle, barnacle-encrusted, its neck sealed with a plug of wax.

He pried it loose, the glass cold and slick in his gnarled hands. Back in the warm, oil-scented quiet of the keeper’s cottage, he held it up to the light. A rolled paper, yellowed with age, was trapped inside. His heart, a rusty engine, gave a surprising lurch.

With a small knife, he worked the wax free and coaxed the paper out. It was a single sheet, the ink faded to a ghostly brown. He smoothed it on the table, his thick fingers trembling slightly.

The script was elegant, a woman’s hand.

*To the Keeper of the Light,*

*My name is Eleanor. I am writing
